**Zero tests met the preregistered polynomial-lead criterion.** All 20 locked test–target pairs showed significant split-clean associations at 64 bits. All five taken forward passed Holm correction at both 80 and 96 bits. Their advantages decayed by roughly half for every four additional product bits. The run finished in 57 minutes.

The five finalists came from the first 64 continued-fraction steps of \(\sqrt{7N}\). They represent three distinct comparisons because the smaller-factor and random-factor labels coincide on the eligible residue class for ranks 1–4. Signed centered advantages below are in parts per million. Negative likelihood ratios favor exponential decay. [Scaling results](</Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ent_c/round2/results/scaling.tsv>)

| Locked readout and target | 64 bits | 80 bits | 96 bits | Fitted \(\beta\) in \(2^{-\beta n}\) | \(2\log(L_{\rm poly}/L_{\rm exp})\) |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Even-index count parity → character of 2 | 450.81 | 29.26 | 1.885 | 0.2463 | −6,416 |
| Low-byte parity → character of 2 | 448.66 | 31.55 | 1.709 | 0.2506 | −3,875 |
| Jacobi readout → smaller factor mod 3 | 136.03 | 8.67 | 0.493 | 0.2499 | −6,086 |

At 96 bits, rank 1 fired on **959 of 224,971,804 clean rows**. Its exact fixed-margin association \(p\) was \(2.55\times10^{-44}\). Its signed **raw agreement advantage** was −54.22 ppm, with exact 95% CI [−119.56, 11.12]. The positive centered effect was 1.885 ppm, rising to 2.086 ppm when flagged rows were included. Independent arithmetic checks verified the continued-fraction certificate on all 223 positive rows in the original 96-bit cohort. [Confirmation estimates](</Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ent_c/round2/results/confirmation.tsv>) · [Exact conditional tests](</Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ent_c/round2/results/conditional_exact.tsv>) · [Split flags](</Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ent_c/round2/results/split_flags.tsv>)

**The power top-up mattered.** Before inspecting confirmation estimates, I registered additional draws to cover the finalists’ eligible residue class. On rank 1’s exact 96-bit clean rows, this raised single-test power for a \(1/n^2\) leak from 23.7% to 82.3%. The measured plant was +157.21 ppm, exact 95% CI [91.87, 222.54], against a planted 108.51 ppm. Power at the first five-test Holm cutoff was 64.7%. At 64 bits the matched weak plant missed the 1% threshold (\(p=0.0312\)). The low-bit sentinel gave \(p=0.430\), and no shuffle control survived discovery. [Matched plant results](</Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ent_c/round2/results/matched_plants.tsv>)

The run screened 20,000 truth-table-distinct tests spanning G1–G7 against 66 targets, with correction across 5.28 million comparisons. It used 599.83 million sampled products. An independent audit caught a rounding error in the initial PNT encoder. After correcting it to the registered floor, I replayed discovery and validation and reproduced both the promotion and lock manifests byte for byte. No PNT or GL target locked. [Full report](</Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ent_c/round2/RESULTS.md>) · [Preregistration](</Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ent_c/round2/PREREGISTRATION.txt>) · [Power addendum](</Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ent_c/round2/POWER_ADDENDUM.txt>) · [Code and replay guide](</Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ent_c/round2/RUNBOOK.txt>)